('prt_f9f69f16f001mBoW62d2EbB8C6', 'msg_f9f69eb60001pfWP5a4iNuy6fw', 'ses_060eeab05ffeKRaLEPC9lrl9WX', 1785085948271, 1785085955586, '{"type":"reasoning","text":"There are 4 commits with \"(conversation-backup)\" in their messages. I need to rewrite them all. Let me use interactive rebase or do it manually. I''ll use `git rebase -i` but since the instructions say not to use `-i`, I''ll do it by cherry-picking and amending each one.\n\nActually, the instructions say \"Do not update git config, skip hooks, use interactive `-i`\". So I shouldn''t use `-i`. Let me do it manually.\n\nLet me rebase from the first commit that needs changing. The base would be `34b164a` (the commit before the ones that need changing).\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. \n\nSo I''ve got four commits to fix: the backup push command fix, the pre-check enhancements with batch mode, the parallel backup execution, and the batch import flow changes. I''ll cherry-pick each one and amend the commit messages to remove the \"(conversation-backup)\" prefix.\n\nActually, I realize I can use `git filter-branch` or `git rebase` with `--exec` to automate this more cleanly. Since I can''t use interactive rebase, I''ll set up a rebase from the base commit and use `--exec` to amend each commit message automatically as it''s replayed.\n\nSetting up the rebase command... I''m realizing the `--exec` approach would apply to every commit, even those that don''t need changes. Since the user wants all `(conversation-backup)` references removed regardless of type, I should use a simpler method that targets only the commits with that substring. Let me go with the cherry-pick and amend approach instead—it''s more straightforward than trying to conditionally modify commits during a rebase.\n","time":{"start":1785085948271,"end":1785085955585}}'),
