e main branch commits with their corresponding source messages, though I''m uncertain whether to include the additional commits from the source that don''t have direct equivalents in the main branch. I''m realizing the user wants me to update the main branch commit messages to match the source project''s commits, so I need to figure out which source commits correspond to which main branch commits and then rebase to fix the messages. The simplest approach is to do an interactive rebase on the last few commits and amend each message to match the source''s wording. I''m realizing the user wants me to align the main branch commit messages with the source project''s recent commits. Looking at the mapping, I need to update three commits: the refactor should become a perf commit about parallel execution, the feat commit needs the conversation-backup prefix and expanded description, and the fix commit needs the same prefix. I''ll use interactive rebase to make these changes.\n\nWait, I''m noticing the conversations-backup branch has commits that don''t exist in main—like the one about only fetching the conversations-backup branch instead of all branches. The main branch is missing these commits entirely, so I need to figure out whether I''m just updating messages or if I need to cherry-pick missing commits first.\n\nLooking at what the user actually asked for, they want me to amend the commit messages on main to match the source project''s latest commits. So I should focus on the three commits that do exist and update their messages accordingly. Let me use interactive rebase to change the commit messages directly.\n","time":{"start":1785085391523,"end":1785085411625}}'),
